a green cruciferous vegetable, with the leaves, buds, and stems all being edible; the buds somewhat resemble broccoli. Rapini is known for its bitter taste, and is particularly associated with Mediterranean cuisine. It is a particularly rich dietary source of vitamin K."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-grelos"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grelos"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Bacalhau à minhota","dish/bacalhau-a-minhota"],["Polvo à lagareiro","dish/polvo-a-lagareiro"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Olive","ingredient/olive"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Bacalhau","ingredient/bacalhau"],["Fried potatoes","ingredient/fried-potatoes"],["Fries","ingredient/fries"],["Olives","ingredient/olives"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rapini","ingredient/rapini"],["Salt cod","ingredient/salt-cod"],["Batatas a murro","ingredient/batatas-a-murro"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Octopus","ingredient/octopus"],["Octopus as food","ingredient/octopus-as-food"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/7/76/Rapini.jpg","w":500,"h":567,"by":"Roedelius","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rapini.jpg"}},"ingredient/chicago-style-relish":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/chicago-style-relish","n":"Chicago-style relish","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Chicago-style relish is a type of pickle relish typically used on Chicago-style hot dogs. It is a sweet and tangy condiment made from finely chopped cucumbers, sugar, vinegar, and other spices. The unique color of the relish, often referred to as \"neon green\", is created by adding blue dye to regular pickle relish. The first use of Chicago-style relish on a hot dog has been attributed to several different restaurants, including Fluky's and Superdawg."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-chicago-style-relish"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago-style_relish"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Celery salt","ingredient/celery-salt"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Hot dog","ingredient/hot-dog"],["Kosher dill pickle","ingredient/kosher-dill-pickle"],["Mustard","ingredient/mustard"],["Mustard (condiment)","ingredient/mustard-condiment"],["Pickled cucumber","ingredient/pickled-cucumber"],["Poppy seed","ingredient/poppy-seed"],["Sport peppers","ingredient/sport-peppers"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Vienna Beef","ingredient/vienna-beef"],["Vienna sausage","ingredient/vienna-sausage"],["White onion","ingredient/white-onion"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/02/GoldCoastChardog.jpg/500px-GoldCoastChardog.jpg","w":500,"h":334,"by":"Wjmummert","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GoldCoastChardog.jpg"}},"ingredient/qishta":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/qishta","n":"Qishta","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Qishta, also known as kishta, kashta, ghishta, ashta or eshta, is a dairy coagulated milk product prepared from heated fresh milk used in different dishes and consumed as a dessert. It is found in Southern Mediterranean cuisines, including Levantine and Egyptian cuisines. It is sometimes scented with orange flower water. Qishta may be served with fruit, used to fill crêpes and pastries, or served with pistachios and sweet syrups. Qishta is \"highly perishable\" and, if improperly prepared or stored, may be a source of foodborne illness such as Listeria. It is similar to other heat-prepared dairy dishes such as kaymak and khoa."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-qishta"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qishta"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Syrian cuisine","cuisine/syrian-cuisine",[["Halawet el jibn","dish/halawet-el-jibn"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Akkawi cheese","ingredient/akkawi-cheese"],["Pistachio","ingredient/pistachio"],["Rose water","ingredient/rose-water"],["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/6d/Arabian_cream_.jpg/500px-Arabian_cream_.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Tahaqdz","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arabian_cream_.jpg"}},"dish/harira":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/harira","n":"Harira","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Moroccan cuisine","dot":"#e46a3f","intro":[["Harira is a traditional North African soup prepared in Morocco and Algeria with many variations, the best-known being Moroccan harira. Harira is popular as a starter, and is also eaten on its own as a light snack. It is mostly served during Ramadan, although it can be made throughout the year."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-harira"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harira"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Moroccan cuisine","cuisine/moroccan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":11,"links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chickpea","ingredient/chickpea"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Lentil","ingredient/lentil"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Vermicelli","ingredient/vermicelli"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"My Moroccan Food","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mymoroccanfood.com","note":"Moroccan recipe blog linked to cookbook author Nargisse Benkabbou, covering breads, soups and tagines.","url":"https://www.mymoroccanfood.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Taste of Maroc","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"tasteofmaroc.com","note":"Moroccan recipes and tutorials from Christine Benlafquih, formerly the Moroccan food writer at The Spruce Eats.","url":"https://tasteofmaroc.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"},{"name":"Salima's Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"salimaskitchen.com","note":"Moroccan section of Salima Benkhalti's blog, drawing on her family cooking.","url":"https://salimaskitchen.com/category/moroccan/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Moroccan cuisine","links":[["Babbouche","dish/babbouche"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Couscous","dish/couscous"],["Kourdass","dish/kourdass"],["Medfouna","dish/medfouna"],["Mhancha","dish/mhancha"],["Pastilla","dish/pastilla"],["Sellou","dish/sellou"],["Tajine lham-lahlou","dish/tajine-lham-lahlou"],["Taktouka","dish/taktouka"],["Tehane","dish/tehane"],["Zaazaa","dish/zaazaa"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/62/Harira_oranaise.png/500px-Harira_oranaise.png","w":500,"h":431,"by":"وهراني","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harira_oranaise.png"}},"dish/matambre":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/matambre","n":"Matambre","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Argentine cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Matambre is the name of a very thin cut of beef in Argentina, Uruguay, Rio Grande do Sul and also Paraguay. It is a rose-colored muscle taken between the skin and the ribs of the steer, a sort of flank steak. It is not the cut known normally in the U.S. as flank steak. Some people refer to it as the \"fly shaker\", because it is the muscle used by the animal to twitch to repel flies and other flying insects."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-matambre"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matambre"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Argentine cuisine","cuisine/argentine-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Familia Kitchen: Argentina","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"familiakitchen.com","note":"Argentine recipes and stories on a Latino family cooking site.","url":"https://familiakitchen.com/destination/argentina/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Argentine cuisine","links":[["Anticucho","dish/anticucho"],["Argentine pizza","dish/argentine-pizza"],["Asado","dish/asado"],["Bara brith","dish/bara-brith"],["Bondiola sandwich","dish/bondiola-sandwich"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Dulce de batata","dish/dulce-de-batata"],["Fugazza","dish/fugazza"],["Jesuita (sandwich)","dish/jesuita-sandwich"],["Jésuite","dish/jesuite"],["Puchero","dish/puchero"],["Rogel (cake)","dish/rogel-cake"],["Salsa golf","dish/salsa-golf"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f1/Matambre1.jpg/500px-Matambre1.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Gesellman","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matambre1.jpg"}},"dish/mirchi-bada":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/mirchi-bada","n":"Mirchi bada","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Mirchi Bada or Mirchi Vada  is a vegetarian fast food dish native to Jodhpur in the Indian state of Rajasthan. The dish is made by stuffing a green chili with indian spices, mashed potato, and gram flour, then it is wrapped in besan batter and fried until golden brown. It is served hot and occasionally eaten with mint and tamarind chutney. Banana pepper and Bhavnagri chillies are used to make mirchi bada."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-mirchi-bada"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirchi_bada"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Gram flour","ingredient/gram-flour"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Swasthi's Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"indianhealthyrecipes.com","note":"Swasthi Shreekanth's long-running blog of everyday Indian home cooking from across the regions.","url":"https://www.indianhealthyrecipes.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Hari Ghotra","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"harighotra.co.uk","note":"UK-based cook Hari Ghotra's recipe library and videos of home-style Indian dishes.","url":"https://www.harighotra.co.uk/indian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Indian cuisine","links":[["Akuri","dish/akuri"],["Aloo paratha","dish/aloo-paratha"],["Amba (condiment)","dish/amba-condiment"],["Baingan bharta","dish/baingan-bharta"],["Bombay potato","dish/bombay-potato"],["Butter chicken","dish/butter-chicken"],["Chaat masala","dish/chaat-masala"],["Chicken Chettinad","dish/chicken-chettinad"],["Dahi vada","dish/dahi-vada"],["Dal dhokli","dish/dal-dhokli"],["Dal makhani","dish/dal-makhani"],["Fafda","dish/fafda"],["Falooda","dish/falooda"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Handvo","dish/handvo"],["Hyderabadi haleem","dish/hyderabadi-haleem"],["Idli","dish/idli"],["Indian restaurant","dish/indian-restaurant"],["Kachumber","dish/kachumber"],["Kidyo","dish/kidyo"],["Kulfi","dish/kulfi"],["Masala chai","dish/masala-chai"],["Mokthuk","dish/mokthuk"],["Naan","dish/naan"],["Osmania biscuit","dish/osmania-biscuit"],["Panipuri","dish/panipuri"],["Pathia","dish/pathia"],["Rabri","dish/rabri"],["Shemai","dish/shemai"],["Tandoori chicken","dish/tandoori-chicken"],["Tandoori roti","dish/tandoori-roti"],["Thali","dish/thali"],["Zarda (food)","dish/zarda-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b3/Mirchi_bada.jpg/500px-Mirchi_bada.jpg","w":500,"h":651,"by":"Hksaisrikar29","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirchi_bada.jpg"}},"ingredient/taboon-bread":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/taboon-bread","n":"Taboon bread","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["A tabun oven, or simply tabun, is a portable clay oven, shaped like a truncated cone. While all were made with a top opening, which could be used as a small stove top, some were made with an opening at the bottom from which to stoke the fire. Built and used even before biblical times as the family, neighbourhood, or village oven, tabun ovens continue to be built and used in parts of the Middle East today. Tabun ovens are distinct from tandoors, but are often mistaken for tandoors. Unlike a tandoor, bread inside the oven is placed on a bed of hot stones, rather than being slapped into walls of the oven. In modern times, this form of oven is common in Jordan and Palestine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-taboon-bread"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taboon_bread"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Arab cuisine","cuisine/arab-cuisine",[["Shawarma","dish/shawarma"]]],["Palestinian cuisine","cuisine/palestinian-cuisine",[["Musakhan","dish/musakhan"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Allspice","ingredient/allspice"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Pine nut","ingredient/pine-nut"],["Saffron","ingredient/saffron"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Laffa","ingredient/laffa"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Lavash","ingredient/lavash"],["Mentha","ingredient/mentha"],["Morkovcha","ingredient/morkovcha"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Pita","ingredient/pita"],["Syzygium","ingredient/syzygium"]]}]},"dish/nam-ngiao":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/nam-ngiao","n":"Nam ngiao","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Thai cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Nam ngiao or nam ngio (Thai: น้ำงิ้ว, pronounced [ná(ː)m ŋíw]) is a noodle soup or curry of the cuisine of the Tai Yai people who live in the northeast of Myanmar, the southwest of Yunnan province, China, and in northern Thailand, mainly in Mae Hong Son Province. The dish has become famous in the northern Thai cuisine. Nam ngiao has a characteristic spicy and tangy flavor."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-nam-ngiao"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nam_ngiao"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Khanom chin","ingredient/khanom-chin"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Thua nao","ingredient/thua-nao"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Hot Thai Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"hot-thai-kitchen.com","note":"Pailin Chongchitnant's recipes and videos, with background on technique and ingredients.","url":"https://hot-thai-kitchen.com","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"SheSimmers","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"shesimmers.com","note":"Bangkok-born writer Leela Punyaratabandhu's blog on Thai home cooking.","url":"https://shesimmers.com/who-is-shesimmers","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Thai cuisine","links":[["Akhuni","dish/akhuni"],["American fried rice","dish/american-fried-rice"],["Boat noodles","dish/boat-noodles"],["Drunken noodles","dish/drunken-noodles"],["Evil jungle prince","dish/evil-jungle-prince"],["Green curry","dish/green-curry"],["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"],["Khanom babin","dish/khanom-babin"],["Khanom chin","dish/khanom-chin"],["Khanom thuai","dish/khanom-t